You already know relative error—the absolute error divided by the true value. Percentage error is just that number multiplied by 100, so it reads as a percent instead of an awkward decimal: {{viz:formula-01}}

Why bother? Because "0.02" doesn't mean much to most people at a glance, but "2%" instantly clicks. Percentages are just a more human-friendly way to talk about relative error. {{viz:anim-01}}

Let's finish the paperclip example from before: relative error was 0.5, so percentage error = 0.5 × 100 = 50%. Ouch—half the paperclip's length off. Compare that to the running track's relative error of 0.0001 → percentage error of 0.01%. Basically perfect.

In a real lab, if you calculate the acceleration due to gravity and get 9.9 m/s² instead of the accepted 9.8 m/s², your percentage error is about 1%—which is honestly a pretty solid result for a hands-on experiment! {{viz:3d-01}}

Watch how percentage error scales directly with relative error—it's the exact same shape, just stretched by 100. {{viz:chart-01}}

Most lab reports, engineering specs, and scientific papers report percentage error because it's the version everyone can understand at a glance. {{viz:text-01}} Calculate a few yourself here. {{viz:simulation-01}}
